$ opengrep scan --help | grep -ni "taint"
192:       --guarded-taint-signatures
193:           Attach branch-condition guards to taint effects and evaluate them
445:       --taint-intrafile
446:           Enable intra-file inter-procedural taint analysis. Supported

  Two options out of 110, and a case-insensitive search of the whole help text
  for 'taint' matches nothing but those two option names and their own
  descriptions - no other option's description mentions taint at all. NEITHER
  OF THE TWO DISABLES TAINT ANALYSIS. --taint-intrafile ENABLES intra-file
  inter-procedural taint and its own text says the fallback is
  'intraprocedural analysis only', not no analysis; --guarded-taint-signatures
  attaches branch-condition guards to taint effects and requires
  --experimental. There is no --no-taint, --disable-taint, --taint-off or
  equivalent at this version, so in this A/B 'taint off' means
  'intraprocedural taint' and a truly taint-free arm is not constructible.

$ opengrep scan --help | grep -A5 -E "^ +--taint-intrafile$"
       --taint-intrafile
           Enable intra-file inter-procedural taint analysis. Supported
           languages: Apex, C, Clojure, C#, C++, Dart, Elixir, Go, Java,
           JavaScript, Julia, Kotlin, Lua, Python, Ruby, Rust, Scala, Swift,
           TypeScript, Visual Basic. Other languages will fall back to
           intraprocedural analysis only.

$ opengrep scan --help | grep -A4 -E "^ +--guarded-taint-signatures$"
       --guarded-taint-signatures
           Attach branch-condition guards to taint effects and evaluate them
           at call sites, dropping effects whose guard is false. Without this
           flag only Clojure keeps the arity guards that implement
           multi-arity dispatch. REQUIRES --experimental

